Replay must never reach the network, not even by mistake.
In [[pyproject.toml]] we therefore give [[pytest]] the options
[[--block-network --allowed-hosts=127.0.0.1,::1,localhost]]: any request to
another host then fails, unless a live run or a recording turns the block off.


\section{Choosing the mode}

The two live options are separate because they carry different risks.
A read test only looks at the data, while a write test changes it.
<<command-line options>>=
def pytest_addoption(parser):
  group = parser.getgroup("ladok3")
  group.addoption(
    "--live", action="store_true",
    help="run the tests against LADOK's test environment, record into a "
         "temporary directory and compare with the cassettes "
         "(attended: may prompt for MFA)")
  group.addoption(
    "--live-writes", action="store_true",
    help="with --live or --record-mode, also run the tests that change "
         "data in LADOK's test environment")
  group.addoption(
    "--live-dir", default=None,
    help="where --live records, as --live-dir=PATH "
         "(default: a private directory under the temporary directory)")
@

A live run is a recording that goes elsewhere.
We set the record mode to [[all]], so every request goes to LADOK, and turn
the network block off.
We have to set the options themselves rather than the fixtures, because
[[pytest-recording]] reads its network block from the options.
Recording needs the pseudonymization key; without it we refuse to start
rather than record anyone's data in clear.
<<test configuration>>=
def pytest_configure(config):
  config.addinivalue_line(
    "markers",
    "mutates: the test changes data in LADOK; reaches LADOK only with "
    "--live-writes")
  config._recorded_dirs = set()
  if config.getoption("--live"):
    config.option.record_mode = "all"
    config.option.block_network = False
    config._live_dir = live_directory(config)
    prepare_live_dir(config._live_dir, Path(str(config.rootpath)).resolve())
  if record_mode_of(config) != "none" \
      and not os.environ.get(vcr_support.KEY_ENV):
    pytest.exit(f"refusing to record: set {vcr_support.KEY_ENV} so that "
                "personal data is pseudonymized", returncode=2)


def record_mode_of(config):
  """Return the record mode, `none` meaning replay."""
  return config.getoption("--record-mode") or "none"
@

The live recordings hold real people's data, pseudonymized, so they go in a
private directory outside the repository rather than in the shared temporary
directory.
The directory name includes a hash of the project's path, so two checkouts
don't share one.
<<test configuration>>=
def live_directory(config):
  """Return the directory that a live run records into."""
  root = Path(str(config.rootpath))
  tag = hashlib.sha256(str(root.resolve()).encode()).hexdigest()[:10]
  return Path(config.getoption("--live-dir")
              or vcr_support.private_dir()
                 / f"{root.name}-{tag}-live-cassettes").resolve()
@

A live run starts with an empty directory, so that recordings from an earlier
run can't hide that a request is no longer made.
Emptying a directory is dangerous if the path is mistyped, so we only empty a
directory that an earlier live run wrote, as shown by a marker file, and we
never accept a directory that contains the project.
<<constants>>=
LIVE_MARKER_FILE = ".pytest-live-cassettes"
<<test configuration>>=
def prepare_live_dir(live_dir, root):
  """Empty `live_dir` for a live run, if an earlier live run made it."""
  if live_dir == root or live_dir in root.parents:
    pytest.exit(f"refusing --live-dir={live_dir}: it contains the project",
                returncode=2)
  if live_dir.exists() and any(live_dir.iterdir()):
    if not (live_dir / LIVE_MARKER_FILE).exists():
      pytest.exit(f"refusing to empty --live-dir={live_dir}: it has files "
                  "that an earlier live run did not write", returncode=2)
    shutil.rmtree(live_dir)
  live_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
  (live_dir / LIVE_MARKER_FILE).write_text(
    "written by pytest --live; safe to delete\n")
@

A [[mutates]] test is skipped when it would reach LADOK, in a live run or
while recording, unless we asked for writes.
In replay it runs: then its writes go to the cassette, not to LADOK.
<<collection rules>>=
def pytest_collection_modifyitems(config, items):
  talks_to_ladok = record_mode_of(config) != "none"
  if not talks_to_ladok or config.getoption("--live-writes"):
    return
  skip_writes = pytest.mark.skip(
    reason="changes data in LADOK; add --live-writes")
  for item in items:
    if "mutates" in item.keywords:
      item.add_marker(skip_writes)
@


\section{Sessions for the tests}

When we talk to LADOK, all tests share one session,
[[ladok3.test_session]].
It reuses the cached login from [[ladok login]] (\cref{CachedSession}), so
a live run usually needs no new MFA prompt.

The login must not end up in a cassette.
The SAML response that the login posts back to LADOK is the signed identity
of whoever logged in, and no scrubber can read it.
We therefore log in before any cassette opens.
[[pytest-recording]] opens a test's cassette in a function-scoped fixture,
and [[pytest]] sets up session-scoped fixtures before function-scoped ones,
so a session-scoped fixture that makes the first request does the login
outside every cassette.
It must be [[autouse]], because a fixture that a test asks for by name may
be set up after the cassette is already open.
When there are no stored credentials, [[ladok3.test_session]] raises
[[RuntimeError]].
The tests that need LADOK are then skipped rather than failed, since the
problem is the machine's set-up, not the code; the fixture itself must not
skip, because skipping an [[autouse]] fixture would skip the offline tests
too.
Any other error is left to fail, because it may well be a bug in the login
code.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def recording(record_mode):
  """True when the tests talk to LADOK: a live run or a recording."""
  return record_mode != "none"


@pytest.fixture(scope="session", autouse=True)
def live_ladok(recording):
  """The logged-in session for LADOK's test environment, or the reason why
  there is none.  Logs in before any cassette opens."""
  if not recording:
    return "replaying"
  try:
    session = ladok3.test_session
    session.user_info_JSON()
  except RuntimeError as err:
    return f"cannot log in to LADOK: {err}"
  return session
@

In replay, the tests get a session that never logs in.
It needs one thing that a logged-in session has: LADOK's [[XSRF-TOKEN]]
cookie, which the write requests send back (\cref{XSRFtoken}).
The cassettes keep the cookie but not its value, so a placeholder is enough.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def replay_ladok():
  """A session for LADOK's test environment that never logs in."""
  session = ladok3.LadokSession("KTH Royal Institute of Technology",
                                test_environment=True)
  session.session.cookies.set("XSRF-TOKEN", vcr_support.SECRET_PLACEHOLDER)
  return session
