\section{Recording}

Each test module gets its own cassette directory under [[tests/cassettes]],
named after the module.
In a live run the same relative path is used, but under the live directory.
We note which directories were written, so that we can scan them and, in a
live run, compare them after the run.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="module")
def vcr_cassette_dir(request):
  module = Path(request.node.path)
  committed = module.parent / "cassettes" / module.stem
  config = request.config
  if config.getoption("--live"):
    target = config._live_dir / committed.relative_to(config.rootpath)
    config._recorded_dirs.add((committed, target))
    return str(target)
  if record_mode_of(config) != "none":
    config._recorded_dirs.add((committed, committed))
  return str(committed)
@

\subsection{Pseudonymization}\label{Pseudonymization}

The responses we record are about real people.
[[vcr_support]] scrubs each response before it is written: secrets such as
cookies and tokens are removed, names, email addresses, usernames and
personnummer are replaced by pseudonyms, and free text is replaced
wholesale, since prose can name anyone.
The pseudonyms are keyed hashes, so the same person gets the same pseudonym
in every cassette, and only someone with the key can link a pseudonym to a
person.
The key, in the environment variable [[TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY]], must therefore
never be committed; keep it in the keyring.
It must also stay the same between recordings, or every pseudonym changes and
a re-recording becomes impossible to review.

[[vcr_support]] already knows LADOK's main field names, such as [[Fornamn]],
[[Personnummer]] and [[SenastAndradAv]].
We adjust its lists in three places.
\begin{description}
\item[[[Uid]] ] is a login name in many services, so [[vcr_support]] replaces
  it.
  In LADOK it is the opaque identifier of a record, which is what lets a
  reader follow a student or a course round from one response to the next,
  and the tests request records by it.
  We keep it.
\item[[[OvrigaMedverkande]] ] (\enquote{other contributors}) lists the
  teachers who took part in grading a result, as free text.
\item[[[Fodelsedata]] ] is the student's date of birth.
\end{description}
<<constants>>=
LADOK_LOGIN_KEYS = vcr_support.LOGIN_KEYS - {"uid"}
LADOK_FREE_TEXT_KEYS = vcr_support.FREE_TEXT_KEYS | {"ovrigamedverkande"}
LADOK_CONTACT_KEYS = vcr_support.CONTACT_KEYS | {"fodelsedata"}
@

The pseudonymizer also notes every original value it replaced, in a private
file outside the repository, so that the scan after the run
(\cref{Drift}) can look for any of them that slipped through elsewhere.
In replay nothing is written, and any key will do.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def pseudonymizer(request, recording):
  key = os.environ.get(vcr_support.KEY_ENV, "")
  originals = Path(os.environ.get(vcr_support.ORIGINALS_ENV)
                   or vcr_support.default_originals_path(
                        request.config.rootpath))
  request.config._originals_path = originals
  return vcr_support.Pseudonymizer(
    (key or "replay-only").encode(),
    originals if recording else None,
    login_keys=LADOK_LOGIN_KEYS,
    free_text_keys=LADOK_FREE_TEXT_KEYS,
    contact_keys=LADOK_CONTACT_KEYS)


@pytest.fixture
def vcr_config(pseudonymizer):
  return pseudonymizer.vcr_config()
