\section{Checks after the run}\label{Drift}

After a run that recorded anything, we scan what was written for personal
data and secrets: every original value that the pseudonymizer replaced, and
anything shaped like an email address, a personnummer or a token.
A hit fails the run and is reported as a file, a line and a redacted stub,
never the value.

After a live run we also compare the fresh recordings with the ones we
replay.
The comparison looks at structure only: status codes, content types, and the
paths and types of the keys in the JSON bodies, never the values.
A removed key, a changed type or a request that is no longer made is
\emph{drift}: the cassettes no longer describe LADOK, and the tests that
replay them may pass on behaviour that LADOK has dropped.
Drift fails the run.
The fix is to adapt the code if needed and then record the affected tests
again with [[--record-mode=rewrite -k]] \emph{test}, never to edit a
cassette by hand.
<<checks after the run>>=
def pytest_sessionfinish(session, exitstatus):
  config = session.config
  pairs = getattr(config, "_recorded_dirs", set())
  if not pairs:
    return
  reporter = config.pluginmanager.get_plugin("terminalreporter")

  def write(message, **markup):
    if reporter:
      reporter.write_line(message, **markup)
    else:
      print(message)

  <<scan the recordings for personal data>>
  if config.getoption("--live"):
    <<compare the live recordings with the cassettes>>
@

The list of originals holds every value the pseudonymizer replaced, and not
all of them identify anyone.
A student's city ([[Postort]]) is replaced as contact data, and the words of
a free-text note are replaced with it.
The first recording found such values hundreds of times: a city in the
names of courses given there, a short word in the translation table, and
[[POST]] as the method of the write requests.
Finding a value in LADOK's own public text says nothing about a person, so
we allow the originals that occur there: in course and code-table names
([[Benamning]] and its [[sv]] and [[en]] texts), in codes ([[Kod]]), in the
translation table ([[Text]]), and as HTTP methods.
Responses from LADOK's catalogue and course-information services
([[kataloginformation]], [[utbildningsinformation]]) hold code tables and
course data, not people, so all of their text counts as public.
A name that happens to be a word in a course title is allowed too, but there
it only names the course.
The cassettes store characters outside ASCII escaped, as in
[[Malm\xF6]], and the scan reads the file as it is stored, so it sees the
part before an escape as a word of its own; we therefore also take the words
of the public text in its escaped form, with the escapes themselves taken
out, or the [[n]] of an escaped line break would stick to the next word.
<<scan the recordings for personal data>>=
originals = vcr_support.read_list(getattr(config, "_originals_path", None))
written_files = [path for _, written in sorted(pairs)
                 for path in vcr_support.iter_files([written])
                 if path.name != LIVE_MARKER_FILE]
allow = public_originals(originals, written_files)
leaks = []
for path in written_files:
  leaks += [(path, where, kind, hit)
            for where, kind, hit in vcr_support.scan_file(path, originals,
                                                          allow=allow)]
if leaks:
  write("PRIVACY: the recordings contain personal data or secrets (values "
        "redacted below). Extend the key lists in testing.nw, delete the "
        "cassettes, and record again.", red=True)
  for path, where, kind, hit in leaks:
    write(f"  {path}:{where}: {kind} {hit}")
  session.exitstatus = 1
@

We collect the public text from the response bodies in the cassettes.
<<constants>>=
PUBLIC_TEXT_KEYS = {"sv", "en", "Text", "Benamning", "Kod"}
HTTP_METHODS = {"GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "PATCH", "HEAD", "OPTIONS"}
PUBLIC_SERVICES = ("/kataloginformation/", "/utbildningsinformation/")
ESCAPE_SEQUENCE = r"\\(?:x[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}|u[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}|U[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}|.)"
<<checks after the run>>=
def public_originals(originals, cassettes):
  """Return the originals that occur in LADOK's public text in the
  cassettes, or that are HTTP methods."""
  text = "\n".join(public_text(cassettes)).lower()
  words = set(re.findall(r"\w+", text))
  escaped = re.sub(ESCAPE_SEQUENCE, " ", yaml.safe_dump(text))
  words |= set(re.findall(r"\w+", escaped))
  phrases = sorted({original.lower() for original in originals
                    if original.lower() not in words}, key=len, reverse=True)
  found = set()
  if phrases:
    pattern = re.compile(r"(?<!\w)(?:" + "|".join(map(re.escape, phrases))
                         + r")(?!\w)")
    found = {match.group(0) for match in pattern.finditer(text)}
  return [original for original in originals
          if original.upper() in HTTP_METHODS
          or original.lower() in words
          or original.lower() in found]


def public_text(cassettes):
  """Yield the public strings in the JSON response bodies of the
  cassettes: all of them from `PUBLIC_SERVICES`, otherwise those under
  `PUBLIC_TEXT_KEYS`."""
  for path in cassettes:
    try:
      interactions = yaml.safe_load(path.read_text())["interactions"]
    except (yaml.YAMLError, KeyError, TypeError):
      continue
    for interaction in interactions:
      try:
        body = json.loads(interaction["response"]["body"]["string"])
      except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        continue
      uri = interaction.get("request", {}).get("uri", "")
      public = any(service in uri for service in PUBLIC_SERVICES)
      yield from strings_under(body, PUBLIC_TEXT_KEYS, inside=public)


def strings_under(data, keys, inside=False):
  """Yield the strings in `data` that sit under one of `keys`."""
  if isinstance(data, dict):
    for key, value in data.items():
      yield from strings_under(value, keys, inside or key in keys)
  elif isinstance(data, list):
    for item in data:
      yield from strings_under(item, keys, inside)
  elif inside and isinstance(data, str):
    yield data
@

<<compare the live recordings with the cassettes>>=
drift = []
for committed, live in sorted(pairs):
  if committed.exists() and live.exists():
    drift += vcr_support.compare_directories(committed, live)
for severity, name, key, message in drift:
  if message != "not re-recorded in the live run":
    write(f"  {severity}: {name} {key}: {message}",
          red=severity == "drift")
if any(severity == "drift" for severity, *_ in drift):
  write("DRIFT: LADOK no longer matches the cassettes. Fix the "
        "code if needed, then record the affected tests again with "
        "--record-mode=rewrite -k <test> and review the diff.", red=True)
  session.exitstatus = 1
else:
  write(f"live: no structural drift ({config._live_dir})", green=True)
@


\section{Recording your own cassettes}\label{RecordingOwn}

To record, we need a login to LADOK's test environment, through
[[ladok login]], and a pseudonymization key.
Any long random string will do as a key, but it must stay the same between
recordings and it must never be written to the repository or printed.
With GNOME Keyring we can create one without ever seeing it:
\begin{minted}{bash}
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32), end='')" \
  | secret-tool store --label='ladok3 test pseudonymization key' \
      service ladok3 key test-pseudonym
\end{minted}
Then we record the cassettes that are missing, writes included, reading the
key from the keyring for this command only:
\begin{minted}{bash}
TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY="$(secret-tool lookup service ladok3 key test-pseudonym)" \
  make test PYTESTFLAGS="--record-mode=once --live-writes"
\end{minted}
A live run takes the key the same way, with [[PYTESTFLAGS=--live]].
The recording fails if the scan afterwards finds personal data
(\cref{Drift}); then extend the key lists in \cref{Pseudonymization}, delete
the cassettes and record again.

The tests use records in KTH's part of the test environment: a test student,
the courses DD2395 and DD1301, their rounds and LAB1 modules,
KTH's organisational unit and a result reporter.
Their UIDs are constants in the tests of the API calls.
At another institution, those constants must be replaced by records there,
and the write tests need a login with the right to report results on the
module they write to.

